[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) holds <ProblemCount ticket={4471} /> reports about dates, merged as one
issue and marked known. They are three problems. Each one has its own fix, and each now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not known. Fixing the parser closes only the first row below. The other two stay open.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

- **parser.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants write the day first, so
  day and month swap: 7 April arrives as 4 July. Every tenant, every channel.
- **hint.** The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it
  says; the form then reads the date the tenant's way, day first. Only the form channel, only tenants
  on booking-v3. The parser fix does not touch it: the form needs the hint its tenant writes.
- **one-day move.** <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="one-day move" /> reports where the date is
  off by one day, not swapped: "Lisbon got Tuesday, Toronto got Monday. Same meeting." That is a
  time zone, not an order of fields. The desk saw them (`desk-notes.json`), but filed them under
  `parser` by tenant.

## Why it merged as one

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports on subject alone. All three
problems arrive as "wrong date", so subject matching cannot tell them apart. Intake's tag cannot
either: it is guessed from the reporter's browser, not from the tenant, and disagrees with the
tenant's cause on many reports in this ticket. Count by what the report describes and where it came
from, not by its subject or its tag.

## How the counts are made

- One id, one report. The export pages overlap, so some reports appear twice; each counts once.
- The cause comes from the tenant (locale and booking form, `causes.ts`), never from `intakeTag`.
- A report the desk read as a one-day move is no swap, so it leaves `parser` (`problems.ts`).

